{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 22,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax. To clear the 32 Msps throughput constraint and the 0.44 us p99 batch bound (32 back-to-back results must finish within 0.44 us of the tick, i.e. the datapath must sustain >= 32 results/us), Fmax must be comfortably above 32 MHz, which a fully pipelined CORDIC can hit. This is the only family that can plausibly satisfy both throughput and the tight batch-latency bound. I sweep data_width 14..28 and n_iter 12..22 to map the accuracy-vs-area curve: n_iter 12..14 with modest guards should already clear max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (accuracy_bits >= 12), while higher n_iter/data_width push accuracy_bits up (HV ref 12) at the cost of luts_plus_ffs (HV ref 4000). Keeping n_iter >= 12 avoids the infeasible low-accuracy corner and keeps the search on the trade-off curve. angle_guard -2..4 and frac_guard 0..4 cover the accuracy levers; rounding both ways since it shifts error slightly and costs little."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 22,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, trading some area (fewer FFs than fully pipelined) for a slightly lower Fmax and higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2). It still produces 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax and it can meet the 32 Msps constraint if Fmax stays above 32 MHz. The m=2..4 range lets the search find the area sweet spot: larger m cuts FFs (helping the min luts_plus_ffs objective) but lowers Fmax and raises latency, which pressures the 0.44 us p99 batch bound. I cap m at 4 so latency stays low enough that 32 back-to-back results still fit in 0.44 us. Same accuracy ranges as pipelined since accuracy is family-independent; this family mainly explores the area/latency trade-off that pipelined cannot (it has no m knob)."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec has two hard throughput/latency constraints (throughput_msps >= 32 and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 for 32 back-to-back results) plus max_abs_err <= 2^-12. Only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can plausibly sustain 32 results/us, so I restrict to those two. The accuracy constraint (accuracy_bits >= 12) is the binding accuracy floor; I set n_iter >= 12 and data_width >= 14 so the search stays on the feasible side of the accuracy constraint and spends budget on the area-vs-accuracy trade-off (HV refs 4000 luts_plus_ffs and 12 accuracy_bits) rather than on infeasible low-accuracy corners. I split the budget 55/45 between pipelined (the throughput/latency-safe baseline that defines the upper accuracy/throughput envelope) and pipelined_m (the area-reduction variant via the m knob that can beat pipelined on luts_plus_ffs while still meeting throughput, provided Fmax and latency hold). Both families share identical accuracy behaviour, so the same data_width/n_iter/guard/rounding ranges apply; the distinguishing dimension is m. This gives a well-covered Pareto front across area, accuracy, and the system latency/throughput bounds within the 100-eval budget."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and covers the objective space effectively. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 1351 LUTs+FFs) meets all constraints with 13.3 bits of accuracy (max_abs_err 0.000102 < 0.000244) and high throughput (93.6 MSPS > 32 MSPS). The front extends to high accuracy (20.6 bits) for those needing more precision. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) clearly points to the 1351 design. Since this is the final round and the front spans the relevant trade-off space (area 1351-4226, accuracy 13.3-20.6 bits) with no obvious gaps or unexplored promising regions, stopping is appropriate."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
